# The SlopOS compiler-fork pin.
#
# `scripts/make_rustc_src.sh` materialises the pinned rustc source tree with
# the patches below applied, and `scripts/check_toolchain_pin.sh` fails if
# any line here stops describing what is on disk.
#
# The channel is not repeated here: it lives in `toolchain/PIN`, held equal to
# `rust-toolchain.toml`, and the source tarball's URL is derived from it. What
# this file adds is the tarball's checksum and four patches.
#
# 0001 makes `x86_64-unknown-slopos` a built-in target rather than a JSON
# file — the one thing bootstrap's `--host` cannot resolve without. 0003
# belongs to the same PR and is separate only because the first bootstrap run
# is what found it: bootstrap maps a cross target's triple to a
# `CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME` by hand, and an unrecognised one falls back to
# `Generic`, which loses `LLVM_ON_UNIX` and with it every `Unix/*.inc` file
# the LLVM port patches.
#
# 0004 lets `llvm.static-libstdcpp` build a `rustc_llvm` that links `c++`:
# bootstrap asks the target's C++ compiler where `libstdc++.a` is and hands
# the directory to `rustc_llvm`, which then links `static=c++` out of it —
# and a SlopOS sysroot has `libc++.a` and no `libstdc++.a`. It names the
# archive for `slopos` as bootstrap already does for `windows-gnullvm`.
#
# The fourth patch is the SlopOS LLVM port a second time, because the port has
# to exist in two llvm-project trees and they are different versions.
# `toolchain/llvm/` cuts it against the 18.1.8 release `toolchain/cxx/PIN`
# names, the tree the C++ runtime is built from; rustc bundles its own LLVM at
# `src/llvm-project`, currently 23.1.1, and that is the one a cross-hosted
# compiler's backend is built from. Not one hunk of the 18.1.8 patch applies
# there — `Triple`'s OS names have moved into a `TripleName.def` table, the
# clang driver's headers have moved out of `clang/lib/Driver/`, and
# `Driver::getInstalledDir()` is gone — so
# `toolchain/llvm-rustc/0001-slopos-support.patch` is that tree's copy. Without
# it a `--host=x86_64-unknown-slopos` run reaches `llvm::Llvm` and stops in
# `llvm/ADT/bit.h` looking for `machine/endian.h`, which is the port's first
# hunk.
